From ca0bba4a54594a21b7565a8fabf91cb0a85d3950 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Si-Qi Liu <liusq@tsinghua.edu.cn>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:03:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] spike: brief-499 stage 1 checkpoint, Symbol loses Copy
 (DOES NOT COMPILE)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Not for main. This is the brief-499 spike's stage-1 tree: Copy removed from
Symbol's derive and the compiler-forced ownership fallout repaired as far as
the run got. rum-core, rum-syntax and rum-runtime compile; rum-stdlib and the
core test targets do not, and the crates after them are unmeasured.

The worker stopped under brief-499's stop-loss after two repair passes. That
rule was mis-keyed and the stop was correct: it said "stop if the same crate
fails twice for the same reason", but a non-Copy migration produces only one
reason by construction, so a monotonically converging repair — 304 distinct
diagnostics, then 80, then 5 — tripped a rule meant to catch a stuck one.

The last five sites are the director's, not the worker's: three key moves in
profiling/report_presence_shapes.rs and two symbol moves into the snapshot
ownership maps in session/session_api.rs, all cold paths.

Every inserted clone is a Symbol clone, which is a memcpy on today's
24-byte Copy-layout representation and therefore costs nothing yet. They
become atomic increments only when stage 2 swaps in Arc<SymbolText>, so the
placement of these clones is what stage 4 has to measure, and none of them
clones an Expr, SharedExpr or SharedArgs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
