From 2a05ca67a4403df9b79d5b59988d34809d604307 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Si-Qi Liu <liusq@tsinghua.edu.cn>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:42:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] docs: brief-502, measure the stack slope before the
 instruction count

brief-501's gate found the result the brief was not looking for: a 48-level
peel recursion that succeeds on stage1 reports StackLimit on the candidate in
debug, while release passes and every type size is unchanged. So the
candidate costs more stack per level, and neither an instruction count nor a
size_of would ever have shown it. That measurement goes first because it can
end the line on its own.

The hypothesis is written in as a hypothesis: stage1's Symbol is non-Copy but
trivially droppable, while the candidate owns an Arc, so the glue becomes an
atomic decrement with a cold dealloc path and every frame able to drop a
Symbol carries more unwind state. The brief says plainly that Expr already
needed drop on stage1, so a needs_drop flip is not the whole story and the
quantity of interest is the size of the glue.

Depth is asked for as a bracket rather than a point, because this project's
records show a ceiling flipping inside a band tens of levels wide.

Two director errors are corrected in it rather than left to recur: the
missing alloc-count feature, which is why brief-501 correctly reported that
the supplied binaries had no counters, and the ZeroMQ gate, which should have
told the worker to run without RUM_ALLOW_NATIVE_ZEROMQ_SKIP and report the
nine as environmental.

