Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The two mechanisms add up cleanly — every residual falls between -0.98 and
+0.66 percentage points across twelve workloads, so this is an attribution
rather than a guess.

On ordinary evaluation the owning representation costs +10.93% by itself and
the equality demotion +3.42%, against +14.26% for both. Arc dominates
everywhere except the sort workloads, where comparison-heavy work puts the
equality demotion ahead (+6.02% against +0.81% at width 16000) — an internal
consistency check the split passes.

That settles A1 against it. V-arc is the cheapest owning variant that can
exist, and it is not even a valid A1: it keeps stage1's dense-id equality,
which is sound only because the interner is still process-global and still
assigns one id per text. Session-scoped ownership destroys that guarantee and
forces the fingerprint equality back, so real A1 pays both halves. The
cheapest floor for any owning Symbol on this kernel is +10.93% on ordinary
evaluation, and this project has rejected representation changes at 2-7% and
5-14%.

Allocation counts confirm the same division: V-arc matches the candidate in
every cell, V-eq matches base and stage1 in every cell.

The tree is restored to the candidate; both variant diffs are kept under
target/brief-503/ with their binaries.

